-## Paradigm Terms
-
-| Term | Definition |
-| --- | --- |
-| **Cybernetic development** | Getting better by steering: act, reality answers, learn, and the next attempt starts further along — run as a deliberate, closed feedback loop. At the system level: sense → decide → act → prove → settle → remember → evaluate → adapt. See [Cybernetic Development](cybernetic-development.md) |
-| **Projection** | A compressed model of the world that preserves some distinctions and collapses others: a theory, map, metric, plan, language, codebase, or story. Observers navigate through projections because no bounded observer possesses reality directly |
-| **Representation** | The observable state through which an observer perceives, reasons, communicates, and acts — the notes, tokens, files, diagrams, and documents that can actually be held and manipulated. The projection is what a representation does (preserve/collapse); the representation is what the observer touches. The only thing any observer directly operates on. See [Representational Settlement](representational-settlement.md) |
-| **Representational settlement** | The process by which a representation is transformed, through role-shaped human-agent control loops, into an artifact that produces consequences, generates evidence, and becomes available for future cognition, operation, governance, or valuation. The canon's replacement for "reification": not an abstraction becoming real, but a representation becoming progressively more consequential. See [Representational Settlement](representational-settlement.md#representational-settlement-defined) |
-| **Artifact ladder** | The four rungs of increasing representational consequence: **cognitive artifacts** (extend the observer — notes, sketches, captures), **information artifacts** (organized for communication or decision — reports, specs, cases), **operational artifacts** (intended to produce action — software, workflows, prompts, policies), and **intellectual assets** (artifacts whose repeated settlement demonstrates durable strategic advantage). Work climbs when settlement keeps paying; climbing on one success is imaginary capability. See [The Artifact Ladder](representational-settlement.md#the-artifact-ladder) |
-| **Resourcefulness** | The measure of a projection: whether repeated settlement demonstrates it expands the observer's capacity to act, settle, and reorganize while reducing unnecessary payment. Replaces "correctness" as the universal criterion |
-| **Payment** | What acting costs: time, attention, money, risk, computation, review burden. Reality asks for payment and returns consequence |
-| **Settlement** | What happens when work actually meets reality and consequence registers: the decision made, the test passed, the customer used it. Deliberately one word at two altitudes — the human judgment and the machine-recorded `SettlementRecorded` event are the same concept at different resolutions |
-| **Residue** | What a settlement leaves behind: the lesson, trace, template, or scar. Captured residue is the raw material of development; uncaptured residue is redone work |
-| **Developmental topology** | The structured accumulation of an observer's residue — what has actually been settled — which determines what is reachable next |
-| **Semantic debt** | The cost accumulated when a projection ages: the compression that once helped now hides distinctions that matter |
-| **Positive disintegration** | Dissolving obsolete projection structure while preserving developmental continuity: keep the residue, retire the compression, recompose |
-| **Metabolization** | Absorbing what works — from another projection, or from repeated settlement — into reliable, lower-cost capability. In the runtime: the promotion of a capability after ≥3 usable settlements |
-| **Better problem** | The terminal state of every loop — because there is no problem-free state. Solving a problem generates new problems, each competing for attention; development is measured by the quality of the problems an observer has earned the right to work on, and each settlement trades current problems for better ones. See [Better Problems](cybernetic-development.md#better-problems) |
-| **Problem upgrade** | The deliberate act of adopting a higher-quality problem made affordable by settlement and freed attention — the last step of the [worker loop](roles/syntelligent-worker.md#the-operating-loop) ("upgrade the problem") and the founder's core act at enterprise scale. Precisely: the adoption of an [opportunity](#paradigm-terms) |
-| **Gap** | The underlying object beneath "problem," "question," and "opportunity": a tension between the current state and a reachable better state that can absorb attention and return development. See [The Gap and Its Three Registers](cybernetic-development.md#the-gap-and-its-three-registers) |
-| **Register** | Which projection of a gap is in use — problem (deficit → repair), question (unknown → inquiry), or opportunity (upside → investment). The register changes which affordances become visible and who can act; it never changes the underlying loop of attention, knowledge, intention, payment, settlement, residue. Reframing across registers is a projection choice, not a change of substance — and it never relaxes the affordance test |
-| **Opportunity** | A gap in the strategic register: the same object as a problem, priced by its upside instead of its pain. The register in which strategic thinking becomes operationally executable — it makes better problems selectable in advance rather than only recognizable in retrospect, and is the compilation target from strategy down to cases |
-| **Doctrine stance** | What the whole canon is: doctrine, not metaphysics. Not "this is how things are" but "this is the most resourceful projection currently affordable for navigating today's attention/knowledge/intention socioeconomy" — adopted deliberately, spent rather than believed, kept only while settlement keeps demonstrating it. Not relativism: reality still arbitrates, through settlement. See [The Doctrine Stance](cybernetic-development.md#the-doctrine-stance) |
