-## Infrastructure Terms
-
-| Term | Definition |
-| --- | --- |
-| **Syntelligence** | The skill of humans and synthetic workers steering together: people supply judgment, machines supply generation, the pair gets better over time. *Syn* means together |
-| **Synthetic worker** | An AI agent that can actually do tasks, not just chat: drafts, researches, writes code, uses tools. A worker made of software |
-| **Syntelligent Infrastructure** | The work environment aligning human judgment, machine generation, domain meaning, context, authority, evidence, memory, settlement, and recomposition. The software substrate for cybernetic development. See [Syntelligent Infrastructure](syntelligent-infrastructure.md) |
-| **Harness** | The rules around an agent: what it may touch, what needs human approval, what proof it must leave. In the stack, **SWE_SEED is the harness** — the development-work harness (routing, hooks, traces, proof). No other repo owns the word |
-| **Settlement runtime** | GodSpeed-Agent's canonical role: classify outcomes, metabolize capabilities, adapt. It does not route or prove |
-| **Context packet** | A citation-bearing bundle of context answering `ContextRequired`. Policy requires at least one citation; uncited context is not context |
-| **Authority check** | Policy evaluation producing a signed allow/deny decision (`AuthorityChecked`) before a consequential action runs |
-| **Proof** | Command-execution evidence gating completion claims. "Done" without proof is theater |
-| **Evidence** | The recorded bundle of proof results (`EvidenceRecorded`) |
-| **Usable / partial / unusable** | The three settlement classifications. Only usable settlements (or properly dampened provisional ones) update capability |
-| **Coherence break** | A policy-gate failure event (`CoherenceBreakDetected`): the loop refused an action that failed its gates |
-| **Memory ledger** | The durable Postgres store behind NATS that validates every event fail-closed. A process's local JSONL buffer is a **local ledger** — a write-ahead buffer that forwards one-way to the memory ledger |
-| **Semantic envelope** | The CEP-0008 canonical event-wrapper concept. The **v1 contract** is its wire profile — the sole cross-process contract, with mandatory provenance |
-| **Provenance** | Who or what produced an event, with which model and inputs. Mandatory on every envelope; an audit trail that cannot answer "who did this" is decoration |
-| **Domain model hash** | The hash-versioned identity of the domain manifest, carried in every loop payload so each event names the version of domain truth it acted under |
-| **Benchmark protocol (CEP)** | The Canonical Evaluation Protocol: specs, schemas, conformance rules, and scorers that measure whether the loop's claims hold on live data |
-| **Executable business meaning** | Domain meaning machines can use and humans can read — DomainForge's product |
-| **Semantic projection** | Compiling one canonical semantic model (`.sea`) into the native formats of mature external ecosystems — CMMN, BPMN, ArchiMate, RDF/OWL, OpenTelemetry conventions, Lean, BAML, DSPy, ZenML, Protobuf — and into the model's own AST for programmatic consumption, instead of re-authoring the same meaning per discipline. The replacement for [semantic translation](#pain-naming-terms). See [Semantic Projection](semantic-projection.md) |
-| **Projection guarantee** | The three gates every projected artifact must pass: **deterministic identity** (same model in, byte-identical artifacts out; all IDs minted deterministically), **semantic traceability** (every artifact stamped with the domain model hash and element IDs, so any downstream form names the version of truth it was compiled from), and **projection consistency** (one producer per artifact; cross-projection references, never copies). Projections never mutate the semantics |
-| **Operator family** | A kind of organizational meaning paired with the mature ecosystem that operates on it — adaptive work → CMMN, ordered process → BPMN, enterprise architecture → ArchiMate, semantic graph → RDF/OWL, runtime observation → OTel, formal verification → Lean, AI representation/optimization/learning → BAML/DSPy/ZenML, service contracts → Protobuf/gRPC, programmatic representation → AST. The unit of projection design: a family, never a one-off exporter |
-| **Semantic IR** | The strategic position semantic projection targets: the canonical intermediate representation of organizational meaning that every discipline's toolchain compiles from — the LLVM pattern applied to meaning instead of code, converting N×M translation into N+M projections. A **keystone platform** claim: winning by making every ecosystem more valuable, not by replacing them. Held as doctrine, earned per ecosystem. See [The Semantic IR](semantic-projection.md#the-semantic-ir) |
-| **Degraded mode** | The edge appliance's safe state when the governance plane is unreachable: mutations blocked, events spooled. An outage response, never a steady state |
-| **Operational semantic residue** | The structured, meaning-bearing evidence left behind when humans, agents, tools, and policies act inside governed loops — the infrastructure form of [residue](#paradigm-terms). "Semantic exhaust" is the casual hook for the same thing. See [Syntelligent Infrastructure](syntelligent-infrastructure.md#operational-semantic-residue) |
-| **Semantic event ledger** | What the memory ledger is, understood as an asset: not a log (which records that something happened) but a record of what happened in relation to domain meaning, authority, evidence, outcome, and capability. The source asset; every dashboard, audit, and view is a projection of it |
-| **Reverse semantic mining** | Deriving candidate domain models, rules, roles, and workflows from observed governed work instead of demanding blank-page upfront modeling. Candidates are promoted into DomainForge/SEA-Forge only under human review |
-| **Organizational misalignment** | The divergence between declared organizational semantics and observed operational behavior, treated as a first-class computable object. Structural, not anomalous. A major SEA-Forge capability. See [Organizational Misalignment, Computable](organizational-misalignment.md) |
-| **Declared reality / observed reality** | What the organization says is true (policies, org charts, approval matrices) versus what the event record shows actually happens. The delta between them is where risk, waste, policy theater, and opportunity all live |
-| **Semantic drift** | Divergence between declared domain meaning and observed behavior over time. Detected as typed categories: bypass, role mismatch, emergent loop, reordering, ownership drift, authority drift, policy conflict |
-| **Authority drift** | The specific drift where declared authority and actual action diverge — who may act versus who does act |
-| **Machine-legible organization** | An organization whose key semantics (ownership, authority, constraints, intent) live in a structured, queryable substrate systems can reason over. Legibility does not require coherence: disagreement, ambiguity, and exception are represented explicitly rather than erased |
-| **Inductive mode** | The governance inversion for when the declared model is performative rather than operative: under human authorization, the observed graph becomes primary and the declared graph is demoted to a hypothesis evaluated against behavior. See [Inductive Mode](organizational-misalignment.md#inductive-mode) |
-| **Accumulated governed meaning** | The moat: the customer's growing semantic event ledger of declared reality, observed reality, authority, evidence, drift, trust, and capability. Not lock-in — customers can export projections; the history is valuable because it is their operational self-understanding |
